<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>PPC management pricing is confusing on purpose. Some agencies charge a percentage of your ad spend, some a flat monthly fee, some both, and many hide setup costs. Here is how the main models work, what a fair service should include, and how to tell if your account is being managed at all.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"sb-panel sb-takeaways"} -->
<div class="wp-block-group sb-panel sb-takeaways"><!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Key takeaways</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list {"className":"sb-ticks"} -->
<ul class="wp-block-list sb-ticks"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Ad spend and management fees are separate. You pay the ad platform directly.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Flat fees are easier to budget. Percentage fees grow with your spend.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>A good manager works on search terms, ads and tracking every week.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Conversion tracking must be set up before any optimisation means anything.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Ad spend vs management fee</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Your ad spend is what you pay Google or Meta for clicks. The management fee is what you pay someone to run the account. Keep them separate: ads should be billed to your own card, in your own account, so you always keep the data and the history.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common pricing models</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:table {"className":"sb-table"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-table sb-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>How it works</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Percentage of spend</td><td>A share of your monthly ad budget</td><td>Fees rise even when results do not</td></tr><tr><td>Flat monthly fee</td><td>A fixed price per month</td><td>Check how many campaigns are included</td></tr><tr><td>Hybrid</td><td>Flat fee plus a smaller percentage</td><td>Complexity, and harder to compare</td></tr><tr><td>One-off audit or setup</td><td>Fixed price for a defined job</td><td>Make sure you get the account, not just a report</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>
<!-- /wp:table -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What good PPC management includes</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Conversion tracking that works.</strong> Without it, optimisation is guesswork (<a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/6308" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Ads: about conversion tracking</a>).</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Search term reviews.</strong> Irrelevant searches added as negative keywords every week.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Ad testing.</strong> New ad copy tested against the current best performer.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Budget and bid management</strong> moved towards campaigns that convert.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Clear reporting</strong> showing spend, conversions and cost per result, not just clicks and impressions.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Signs your account is not being managed</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>The change history shows almost no activity for weeks.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Search terms include obviously irrelevant phrases.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Every campaign uses broad match with no negative keywords.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Reports show clicks and impressions but never conversions.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>You do not have admin access to your own ad account.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Start with an audit</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you already run ads, a <a href="/services/ppc-management/#packages">Google Ads audit</a> is the cheapest way to find wasted spend before paying for ongoing management. If you advertise on Facebook and Instagram too, see our <a href="/services/facebook-ads/">Facebook ads management</a>.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Questions to ask before hiring anyone</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Will the ads run in my own account, and will I have admin access?</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Exactly what work happens each week, and how will I see it?</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Is conversion tracking included, and who checks it is accurate?</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>What is the notice period, and what happens to my account if I leave?</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Do you charge a percentage of spend, and if so, is it capped?</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A good provider answers all five without hesitation. If the answers are vague, keep looking.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"sb-faq","layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group sb-faq"><!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>What is a reasonable PPC management fee?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>It depends on account size and complexity. For small accounts, flat monthly fees are usually clearer and better value than a percentage of spend.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>How much should I spend on Google Ads?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Enough to get meaningful data, typically enough for at least 20 to 30 clicks a day in your main campaign. Start small, measure, then scale what converts.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Should I own my Google Ads account?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Always. Your account holds your data and history. Agencies should work in your account as users, not run ads from theirs.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>How long before PPC campaigns perform well?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Expect a learning period of two to four weeks, then gradual improvement as search terms, bids and ads are optimised.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

